text:
[MAJOR] Every drug-modal open leaks a WebGL context and installs a permanent, uncancellable requestAnimationFrame loop — `initModal3DCanvas` constructs a fresh `new THREE.WebGLRenderer(...)` on every invocation (app.js:688) and appends its canvas to the container (app.js:691). It is invoked from `openDrugModal` (app.js:648-652) on every card click. `closeDrugModal` (app.js:655-658) does nothing but `modal.classList.remove('active')` -- it does not call `renderer.dispose()`, `forceContextLoss()`, `geometry.dispose()`, or `material.dispose()`, and `grep` confirms the token `dispose` does not appear anywhere in the file. The animation loop is worse. app.js:763-772 reads:

```js
function animateModal3D() {
    if (!modalThreeRenderer) return;
    requestAnimationFrame(animateModal3D);
    if (modalThreeMeshGroup) {
        modalThreeMeshGroup.rotation.y += 0.006;
        modalThreeMeshGroup.rotation.x += 0.003;
    }
    modalThreeRenderer.render(modalThreeScene, modalThreeCamera);
}
```

Its only exit condition is `!modalThreeRenderer`. `grep -n "modalThreeRenderer" app.js` returns exactly six lines (410, 688, 689, 690, 691, 764, 770) and the variable is never assigned `null` or `undefined` anywhere. After the first modal open the guard is therefore unsatisfiable, and every subsequent open adds one more permanently self-rescheduling loop. Because the loop closes over module-level globals rather than its own renderer, all N surviving loops read the *current* `modalThreeRenderer` and `modalThreeMeshGroup`, so after N opens the mesh group is rotated N × 0.006 rad per frame and `render()` is called N times per frame on the same scene. Each `initModal3DCanvas` call also constructs a `TubeGeometry` with `seqLength * 3` tubular segments (app.js:732), a `SphereGeometry` (:742), ~seqLength/2 `MeshPhongMaterial` instances (:751), and a pocket sphere (:757) -- all abandoned without disposal when the container is cleared by `container.innerHTML = ''` at app.js:678. Consequence: The symptom is deterministic and user-visible: the 3D model spins twice as fast after the second modal open, three times as fast after the third, and so on -- so the animation itself advertises the bug. Browsers cap concurrent WebGL contexts (Chrome at 16); past that limit the oldest context is force-lost and its canvas goes blank, so a user who inspects all 25 candidates in one session will find the 3D panel dead for the last nine, with `WARNING: Too many active WebGL contexts` in the console and no explanation in the UI. Frame time degrades linearly with the number of opens because N redundant draw calls execute per frame. Three.js documents explicitly that GPU-side resources are not reclaimed by JavaScript garbage collection and that `dispose()` must be called; abandoning geometries and materials leaks VRAM. On a page whose stated purpose is browsing 25 candidates in sequence, this is not a theoretical leak -- it is the primary usage pattern.
